scope: |
  Option A, chosen by the project owner: `Any` stays `crate::value::Value` and gains an `Obj(name,
  fields)` variant, so a case-class value can live in an `Any`. The reason I first rejected A was
  wrong -- the types ARE available, in the case-class DECLARATION, so no inference is needed.
  The trick that keeps it bounded: coercions are TOTAL, via traits implemented for both `Value` and
  the concrete type, so the walker only needs the TARGET type (always declared) and never the
  source type.
  Boundaries: construction arg, destructuring bind, call arg, return.
next: Value::Obj + total coercion traits in the runtime template, then ctorMap field TYPES
